{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Toulouse and the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n* **Early Life and Succession** * \nRaymond III was born in 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. He was a vassal of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. During his early years as Count he faced several challenges including the decline of the Crusader states and the rise of Muslim powers in the region.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures and had a daughter with her. He also married a woman named Alice of Jerusalem and had a son with her named Bohemond. Bohemond was his heir and would go on to succeed him as Count of Tripoli.\n\n* **Military Campaigns and Crusades* \nRaymond III participated in several military campaigns during his time as Count of Tripoli. In 1164 he joined the Second Crusade and participated in the Battle of al-Buqaia. He also participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177. Raymond III was a key player in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a crucial role in several battles.\n\n* **Relationships with Muslim Leaders* \nRaymond III had a complex relationship with Muslim leaders in the region. He was a vassal of the Kingdom of Jerusalem but also had to deal with the rise of Muslim powers in the region. In 1179 he formed an alliance with the Ayyubid leader Saladin who would go on to conquer much of the region.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his son Bohemond. The County of Tripoli was eventually conquered by Saladin in 1190. Raymond III played a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states and his legacy would be remembered for centuries to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.021772763}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00021634146}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0